---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Equation from '../../../components/viz/Equation.astro';
import ScientificFigure from '../../../components/viz/ScientificFigure.astro';
import PubItem from '../../../components/ui/PubItem.astro';
import { software } from '../../../data/software';
import { byId } from '../../../data/publications';
import { url } from '../../../i18n/utils';
import fig from '../../../assets/figures/pysat-3d-abel.png';
const s = software.find((x) => x.slug === 'pysat-abel')!;
const paper = byId('littin2024sat')!;
const jsonld = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode', name: 'pysat-abel',
  description: s.summary, programmingLanguage: 'Python', codeRepository: 'https://gitlab.coria-cfd.fr/littinm/pysat',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Mijail Littin' }, version: '0.1.3',
  citation: `https://doi.org/${paper.doi}`,
}];
---
<Base title="pysat-abel" math description={s.summary} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <header class="page-head frame">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href={url('/software/')}>Software</a> · Python</p>
    <h1><code class="h1code">pysat-abel</code></h1>
    <p class="lede">{s.summary}</p>
    <ul class="links head-links">{s.links.map((l) => <li><a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a></li>)}<li><a href={`https://doi.org/${paper.doi}`}>Paper (Fuel 2024)</a></li></ul>
  </header>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="label row__label">What it solves</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>A camera looking at an object with rotational symmetry (a flame, a jet, a plasma) records integrals along lines of sight. Recovering the local radial profile from them is the Abel inversion problem, and a direct inversion amplifies measurement noise near the axis. When the medium also absorbs part of the light on its way to the camera (signal trapping, or self-absorption), the recorded signal is attenuated and a plain Abel inversion gives biased values.</p>
        <p>SAT represents the unknown radial profile <em>κ(r)</em> as a cubic spline with non-negative knot values and zero slope on the axis. The spline is fitted so that its projection matches the measurement, with a penalty on its curvature that suppresses noise:</p>
        <Equation tex={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\hat\kappa &= \arg\min_{\kappa}\, J(\kappa), \\ J(\kappa) &= \int \big[S_{\mathrm{mod}}(x;\kappa) - S_{\mathrm{exp}}(x)\big]^2\,\mathrm{d}x \\ &\quad + \mu \int_0^R \kappa''(r)^2\,\mathrm{d}r,\end{aligned}`} />
        <Equation tex={String.raw`S(x) = 2\int_x^\infty \frac{\kappa(r)\, F(x,r)}{\sqrt{r^2-x^2}}\, r\, dr .`} />
        <p>The factor <em>F(x, r)</em> accounts for trapping. It is computed from the extinction profile, itself obtained first by inverting a line-of-sight attenuation measurement (for which <em>F</em> = 1).</p>
        <p>The <a href={url('/learn/inverse-problems/abel-transform/')}>interactive Abel module</a> introduces the underlying problem, and the flame tab of the home-page figure applies SAT to a simulated flame.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <ScientificFigure src={fig} alt="Diagram: a line-of-sight integrated map of an ethylene flame is converted by the spline-based Abel transform into a reconstructed radial map, shown as a revolved three-dimensional field." caption="An ethylene flame: the line-of-sight map recorded by a camera (left) and the radial map reconstructed with the spline-based Abel transform, shown revolved in three dimensions." credit="Figure by M. Littin, from the pysat-abel documentation." sizes="(min-width: 50rem) 36rem, 100vw" />
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="label row__label">Install</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <pre><code>python -m venv pysat-env
source pysat-env/bin/activate
pip install pysat-abel</code></pre>
        <p class="meta">Current release 0.1.3 (May 2025). Dependencies: NumPy, SciPy, joblib and <code>abel</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="label row__label">Minimal usage</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <pre><code>import numpy as np
from pysat.modules import abel_inversion

# tau: transmissivity across the object (1D, centred on the axis)
r, kext = abel_inversion(tau, data_type="transmissivity", nknots=15, lambda_=1e6)

# emission profile, corrected for trapping with the extinction just recovered
r, J = abel_inversion(s_emi, data_type="emission",
                      apply_correction=True, kext=kext)</code></pre>
        <p><code>abel_inversion</code> returns the radial axis and the recovered coefficient. <code>nknots</code> sets the flexibility of the spline and <code>lambda_</code> the weight of the curvature penalty. The same call handles transmissivity, emission and scattering data. The package documentation includes a tutorial notebook with synthetic data.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section frame">
    <div class="row">
      <h2 class="label row__label">Cite and license</h2>
      <div>
        <PubItem pub={paper} compact />
        <p class="meta lic">License: {s.license}. {s.licenseNote}</p>
        <p class="meta">Authors (PyPI metadata): M. Littin, A. Poux, G. Lefevre, M. Mazur, F. Escudero, A. Fuentes, J. Yon.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .h1code { font-size: 0.9em; background: none; padding: 0; }
  .head-links { margin-top: var(--s-4); }
  .head-links li + li::before { content: none; }
  .head-links li:not(:last-child)::after { content: '·'; color: var(--ink-3); padding: 0 0.5em; }
  .lic { margin-top: var(--s-4); }
</style>
